==================================================================================================================
1. Delta N_eff: WHEN does the relic count as radiation?
==================================================================================================================
  relic mass 11.3 eV
  at recombination (z = 1090): T_gamma = 0.2562 eV, a decoupled species sits at T = 0.1829 eV, so m/T = 61.8
  at BBN (T ~ 1 MeV): m/T = 1.13e-05
  [PASS] A1 (IN THE CONDITIONAL'S FAVOUR, and the repository's note is too strong) an 11.3 eV relic is DEEPLY non-relativistic at recombination (m/T = 68), so it does NOT contribute to the CMB's N_eff at all -- it contributes to the matter density, which is the whole point.  The Delta N_eff = 1 constraint applies at BBN, where it is far looser   (m/T = 62 at recombination (non-relativistic, counts as matter) versus 1.1e-05 at BBN (relativistic, counts as radiation))
  Planck's N_eff = 2.99 +- 0.17 is a CMB-epoch measurement and a non-relativistic species does not enter it.
  BBN measures N_eff = 2.9 +- 0.3 or so; a fully thermalised extra species gives Delta N_eff = 1, i.e. ~2-3 sigma.
  So this is a TENSION at BBN, not the clean CMB exclusion the repository recorded.  Corrected in the conditional's
  favour, and it does not save the conditional -- part 2 does the killing.

